# Copilot Instructions

## Context efficiency

Minimise unnecessary context gathering.

When starting a new task:
1. Read README.md and PLAN.md first, if they exist.
2. Use PLAN.md to identify the current NEXT task.
3. Inspect only the files directly relevant to that task.
4. Expand to additional files only when required to understand dependencies or implement the task correctly.

Do not automatically:
-   scan or analyse the entire repository;
-   read every file to understand the project;
-   review the full git history;
-   perform broad repository searches;
-   audit unrelated code;
-   investigate unrelated branches or previous work;
-   read large files unless they are relevant to the task.

If the relevant files can be identified directly, open those files rather than exploring the repository broadly.

## Plan and implement in one session

If a request asks for a plan and the user has not said to wait for approval, write the plan into PLAN.md and then implement it in the same session, so the user does not need a second session. Stop after the plan only when the user explicitly asks to approve it first, or when the plan has open questions that change the design.

Always state clearly in the final report whether code was changed or only a plan was written.

## Implementation

Work on one PLAN.md task at a time.

Before changing code:
-   understand the task and its acceptance criteria;
-   inspect the smallest practical set of relevant files;
-   preserve existing behaviour unless the task requires changing it.

When implementing:
-   make focused changes;
-   avoid unrelated refactoring;
-   avoid adding unnecessary dependencies;
- follow existing conventions in the files being modified;
- do not investigate unrelated problems unless they block the current task.

## Testing

Run the smallest relevant tests first.

Run broader tests only when:
-   the change could reasonably affect other functionality;
-   dependencies make broader validation necessary; or
-   the focused tests reveal a problem requiring wider investigation.

Do not run expensive or exhaustive test suites automatically when focused validation is sufficient.

## Plan maintenance

After completing a task:
1. Verify the acceptance criteria.
2. Mark the task DONE.
3. Update PLAN.md with any important implementation decision that a future session needs.
4. Identify the next unfinished task as NEXT.
5. Keep PLAN.md concise. Do not turn it into a detailed development log.

## Session completion
At the end of the task, briefly report:
-   what was changed;
-   what was tested;
-   any unresolved issue;
-   what is NEXT.
Do not begin the next
PLAN.md task unless explicitly asked.
